 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S444-018\",\n \"clauses\": {\"distinguish\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F13\", \"template\": \"{statement}. True or False?\", \"reasoning\": \"F13 is the question text. Its statement slot draws from F07 to F12, true and false even/odd claims that a learner must judge, which is the act of distinguishing.\"},\n \"even_and_odd_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"Dividing {d} by 2 gives an exact whole number, so {d} is an even number\", \"reasoning\": \"F08 names both even and odd numbers in its claim. Its d slot repeats the dividend, which prints both even and odd values, so the object of the lc (even and odd numbers) is produced.\"},\n \"division\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F03\", \"template\": \"Divide {n} by 2: how many 2s fit into {n}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F03 names division as the task. Its n slot repeats the dividend over 1 to 90, which is within the grade 2 number_format range, and division appears in cumulative_vocab.\"},\n \"divisor_2\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"{d} ÷ 2 comes out exactly, so {d} is an even number\", \"reasoning\": \"F11 expresses the divisor as 2 in every render. Its d slot repeats the dividend, so the divisor clause is produced.\"}},\n \"failing_families\": [\"F09\"],\n \"failing_templates\": [\"{d} can be divided into 2 equal parts, so {d} is an even number\"],\n \"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n \"reasoning\": \"F09 is the only family at fault. Its wording says 'equal parts' without 'whole', while sibling F10 says 'equal whole parts'. F09's d slot repeats the dividend, and its printed_runs include odd values (9, 17, 19, 25 and others). For those odd values the statement can be read as literally true, since an odd number can be split into two equal halves. The stem then labels the number even, so a grade 2 learner could key it True when the generator intends False. This is misleading, and the stem is unclear at this grade. The other families (F07, F08, F10, F11, F12) also print statements that are false for some values, but as true/false claims their truth is unambiguous, so they are not faults. F01 to F04 are correct for their printed values: F01 and F04 print only odd or even values respectively, F02 is correct arithmetic for every dividend (including 0 as half for dividend 1), and F03 is a correct division prompt. The distractor families F05 and F06 are the words even and odd, which are in EVEN_ODD_LABELS and in the lc's vocabulary. No family uses NOT_YET_KNOWN vocabulary.\"}",
